      <section id="ai-agent" class="cs-section">
        <div class="cs-section-label">01 · AI Interaction Design · Content Design · Research · Engineering</div>
        <h2 class="cs-heading">Designing an AI Support Agent Customers Can Trust</h2>

        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">Result</span>
          <p><strong>3x increase in chatbot-resolved cases.</strong> Customers weren't engaging with our AI agent, and when they did, it often failed them. I studied how customers actually use the agent to get help, then redesigned what it learns from: the content, the conversation flow, and the data it can access.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-note"><strong>Role:</strong> UX Engineer, user research, owning content design, interaction design, QA, and integration <br><strong>Tools &amp; methods:</strong> Forethought, Django CMS, Figma, LLM-assisted analysis, qualitative and quantitative research</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">The problem</h3>
        <p>Our AI support agent was only as good as the content behind it, and customers could tell. Click rates on the AI chat started low, a sign that customers didn't see it as a reliable way to get help. Behind the scenes, support articles were written for people skimming a page, not for a model retrieving an answer: the same fact appeared in different formats across hundreds of articles, key details were buried in PDFs, and the agent often missed which product a customer owned. The result: vague answers, dead ends, and customers giving up on chat to open a case.</p>

        <div class="cs-callout">
          <span class="callout-label">HMW</span>
          <p>How might we make our AI agent understand the question, find the right answer, and deliver it in a way customers trust?</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Process</h3>
        <div class="cs-improvements">
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">01</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Auditing the agent's failures</strong>
              <p>I reviewed real customer conversations where the agent failed or escalated, and used AI-assisted analysis to cluster them into recurring failure types: missing content, unclear content, wrong product context, and answers locked in documents the agent couldn't read.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">02</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Designing content for machines and people</strong>
              <p>I created content standards so every article follows a predictable structure: consistent headings, one idea per section, and reusable components for warnings, steps, and required supplies. Structured content is easier for the AI to retrieve accurately and easier for customers to scan.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">03</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Improving how the agent captures context</strong>
              <p>I redesigned the conversation flow so the agent identifies key context, like which product the customer is using, before answering. That narrows retrieval to the right articles instead of a best guess across the whole catalog.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">04</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Unlocking data trapped in PDFs</strong>
              <p>Product settings lived in PDFs the agent couldn't use reliably. I built a lookup API connected through the agent's action builder, so the agent can answer settings questions directly from source data. The rule I designed around: the agent never invents names or values. Every answer traces back to the source.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">05</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Testing and iterating</strong>
              <p>I tested the agent against real customer questions before and after each change, tracking where answers improved and where gaps remained.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-note">Due to NDA, parts of this process are restricted. I'm happy to walk through the details in conversation.</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Key decisions</h3>
        <div class="cs-insights">
          <div class="insight-item">
            <span class="insight-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Fix the content, not the model</strong>
              <p>Tuning prompts only goes so far when the source content is inconsistent. Restructuring the content fixed the root cause, and it improved the support site for human readers at the same time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="insight-item">
            <span class="insight-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Accuracy over coverage</strong>
              <p>For product settings, a wrong answer is worse than no answer. I designed the agent to answer only from source data and hand off when it can't, which protects customer trust.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="insight-item">
            <span class="insight-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Lightweight over heavyweight</strong>
              <p>I hosted the lookup API on a developer-friendly cloud platform, so the support and documentation team can update the data without an engineer.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Solution</h3>
        <div class="cs-findings">
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>AI-readable content standards</strong></div>
            <p>Structured content standards applied across the knowledge base.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Context-aware conversation flow</strong></div>
            <p>Captures product context before answering.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Source-faithful lookup API</strong></div>
            <p>Answers product settings questions directly from source data.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Fallback to a person</strong></div>
            <p>A clear path to a human when the agent can't answer confidently.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Ongoing test-and-improve loop</strong></div>
            <p>Based on real customer questions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Impact</h3>
        <div class="cs-results-grid">
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">↑</span>
            <strong>3x increase</strong>
            <p>in chatbot-resolved cases</p>
          </div>
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">✓</span>
            <strong>Accurate, product-specific answers</strong>
            <p>without waiting for a human</p>
          </div>
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">✦</span>
            <strong>Shared content standards</strong>
            <p>now serve both the AI agent and the support site</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">What I learned</span>
          <p>Designing for AI is content design. The biggest gains didn't come from tuning the model; they came from giving it clean, structured, trustworthy content and the right context.</p>
          <p>I also learned that a correct answer isn't the same as a useful one. Designing AI interactions means designing the output for the person reading it: is it specific to their product, short enough to act on, and honest about what it doesn't know? Success isn't whether the AI responded. It's whether the customer left with their problem solved.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="routing" class="cs-section">
        <div class="cs-section-label">02 · UX Research · Service Design · AI Interaction · Engineering</div>
        <h2 class="cs-heading">From Phone Calls to Self-Service: Rethinking Support Routing</h2>

        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">Result</span>
          <p><strong>30% reduction in time to resolution and cases opened.</strong> Customers called support by default, even for the simplest questions AI could answer in minutes. Through research, I found out why and redesigned the routing so AI chat comes first and a person is always reachable.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-note"><strong>Role:</strong> UX Engineer, leading research, strategy, and design <br> <strong>Methods:</strong> Journey mapping, user personas, heatmap analysis, user testing, prototyping, competitive analysis</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">The problem</h3>
        <p>With Form Studio 1 expected to reach hundreds of thousands of customers, our support team couldn't answer every question by phone. Yet customers were going straight to the phone, even for the simplest questions our AI agent and support content could answer in seconds. Phone numbers appeared across many pages with inconsistent rules, so customers took the most visible path rather than the fastest one. That meant longer waits for customers and a heavier load on the support team.</p>
        <p>The business need was clear: reserve phone support for the customers who truly need it, and give everyone else a faster path.</p>

        <div class="cs-callout">
          <span class="callout-label">HMW</span>
          <p>How might we guide customers to the fastest path to an answer, without hiding help from people who truly need a human?</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Process</h3>
        <div class="cs-improvements">
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">01</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Understanding where people go for help</strong>
              <p>I mapped the full customer support journey and analyzed heatmaps to see where customers clicked when they needed support. I then ran user testing to understand why they chose each path.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">02</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Auditing every entry point</strong>
              <p>I audited every place a phone number appeared across the website and found conflicting visibility rules between pages, plus a key customer journey that had never been designed for.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">03</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Learning from the best</strong>
              <p>I studied how leading support experiences, including Apple Support, guide customers toward self-service while keeping a clear path to a person.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-note">Due to NDA, detailed process artifacts are restricted. I'm happy to walk through the work in conversation; feel free to reach out.</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Key decision</h3>
        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">Guide, don't hide</span>
          <p>Removing the phone number entirely would have pushed cases down on paper while frustrating customers and eroding trust. I chose contextual visibility instead: AI chat leads, and the phone number appears where a person is truly the right answer.</p>
        </div>

