        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Solution</h3>
        <div class="cs-findings">
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Contextual phone visibility</strong></div>
            <p>Shown only in the right contexts, instead of everywhere.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>AI chat as the primary path</strong></div>
            <p>Positioned as the most visible way to get help.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Clear escalation to a person</strong></div>
            <p>When AI and self-service can't solve the problem.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="finding">
            <div class="finding-header"><span class="finding-icon" style="color:#ff6101">✓</span><strong>Scrape-resistant contact details</strong></div>
            <p>A technical approach that reveals contact details contextually.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Impact</h3>
        <div class="cs-results-grid">
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">↓</span>
            <strong>30% reduction</strong>
            <p>in time to resolution and cases opened</p>
          </div>
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">↑</span>
            <strong>Faster answers</strong>
            <p>customers reach answers faster through AI chat and self-service</p>
          </div>
          <div class="result-card">
            <span class="result-icon">✦</span>
            <strong>More time for complex issues</strong>
            <p>support agents spend more time on issues that need a human</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">What I learned</span>
          <p>Deflection only works when it feels like help, not a wall. Pairing a strong AI-first path with an honest route to a person built trust instead of frustration.</p>
          <p>AI support is new, and customer expectations of it are still forming. Designing well here means watching closely how people's mental models shift and adapting just as quickly.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="migration" class="cs-section">
        <div class="cs-section-label">03 · Engineering · UX Research · Information Architecture · QA · AI-Assisted Workflows</div>
        <h2 class="cs-heading">Rebuilding the Support Site: Salesforce to Django</h2>

        <div class="cs-callout highlight">
          <span class="callout-label">Result</span>
          <p><strong>1,500+ articles migrated · 20+ new components.</strong> We had to move 1,500+ support articles off Salesforce. Instead of copying them by hand, I built a pipeline that migrated and restructured them automatically, designed and engineered the new site, and built QA tools to launch it reliably.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-note"><strong>Role:</strong> Full stack engineer, automation pipeline, UX research and design, system design and QA <br> <strong>Stack:</strong> Python, TypeScript, React, JavaScript, headless CMS, Salesforce, JSON, CI/CD, Figma</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">The problem</h3>
        <p>Our products and documentation were growing fast, and our support site couldn't keep up. It ran on Salesforce Knowledge, which limited design flexibility and made content hard to structure, reuse, and keep consistent. With an AI agent now answering customer questions from that same content, consistency wasn't a nice-to-have: the agent could only be as accurate as the data it pulled from.</p>
        <p>Moving to Django meant migrating more than 1,500 articles and documents, from repair guides to troubleshooting content, without losing formatting, media, or accuracy. Doing it by hand would have taken months and introduced errors.</p>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Process</h3>
        <div class="cs-improvements">
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">01</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Automating the migration</strong>
              <p>I built an automated pipeline that converts Salesforce Knowledge articles into Django-compatible structured JSON. It maps each piece of content to the right CMS component (images, warnings, videos, required supplies) and handles edge cases like embedded YouTube videos and hosted media. I used AI-assisted workflows to speed up mapping rules and handle messy legacy content.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">02</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Rebuilding the information architecture</strong>
              <p>Before designing a single page, I ran user research to understand how customers look for help content. The old structure reflected how our teams organized information, not how customers searched for it, so I rebuilt the information architecture around customers' mental models.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">03</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Designing the new support site</strong>
              <p>I prototyped the new experience in Figma, from wireframes to high-fidelity designs, focused on making articles easier to scan and navigate.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">04</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>Engineering new components</strong>
              <p>I built 20+ reusable, token-based components so the design system and production code stay in sync, and wrote safe, repeatable scripts for updating content at scale.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="improvement-item">
            <div class="improvement-number">05</div>
            <div class="improvement-content">
              <strong>QA at scale</strong>
              <p>With 1,500+ articles, manual checking wasn't enough. I built Chrome extensions to scan pages for broken links and clean up staging URLs automatically, catching issues before launch.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="cs-subheading">Key decisions</h3>
        <div class="cs-insights">
          <div class="insight-item">
            <span class="insight-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Restructure, don't lift-and-shift</strong>
              <p>Copying articles as-is would have been faster up front but would have carried every inconsistency into the new site. Converting them into structured components made the content reusable, and later made it readable for our AI agent.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="insight-item">
            <span class="insight-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Safe to rerun</strong>
              <p>I built every script to be repeatable, with a dry-run mode, so the team could preview changes and rerun migrations without breaking live content.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

